{"title":"2002 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 16","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_WhyMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by WhyMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Right isosceles triangles are constructed on the sides of a 3-4-5 right triangle, as shown. A capital letter represents the area of each triangle. Which one of the following is true?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/b/afbe72ee3e1ba66d7e02b6fd47981276584473cd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] /* AMC8 2002 #16 Problem */ draw((0,0)--(4,0)--(4,3)--cycle); draw((4,3)--(-4,4)--(0,0)); draw((-0.15,0.1)--(0,0.25)--(.15,0.1)); draw((0,0)--(4,-4)--(4,0)); draw((4,0.2)--(3.8,0.2)--(3.8,-0.2)--(4,-0.2)); draw((4,0)--(7,3)--(4,3)); draw((4,2.8)--(4.2,2.8)--(4.2,3)); label(scale(0.8)*&quot;$Z$&quot;, (0, 3), S); label(scale(0.8)*&quot;$Y$&quot;, (3,-2)); label(scale(0.8)*&quot;$X$&quot;, (5.5, 2.5)); label(scale(0.8)*&quot;$W$&quot;, (2.6,1)); label(scale(0.65)*&quot;5&quot;, (2,2)); label(scale(0.65)*&quot;4&quot;, (2.3,-0.4)); label(scale(0.65)*&quot;3&quot;, (4.3,1.5));[/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"185\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/d/86d80d1a2e47fababd6062c49ced0ae8605e8e32.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ X+Z=W+Y\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ W+X=Z\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 3X+4Y=5Z\\qquad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"517\" height=\"17\" /> \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/7/667511c75a5844ca2218c65431b999f910dc0fd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(D)}\\ X+W=\\frac{1}{2}(Y+Z)\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ X+Y=Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"352\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The area of a right triangle can be found by using the legs of triangle as the base and height. In the three isosceles triangles, the length of their second leg is the same as the side that is connected to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/1/af1a2cb4ff1720f169ddde1726c1a5a1ab816ae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3-4-5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" /> triangle. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/a/97a002b0821ad620a94798b792bb7a50b8d7979c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} W&amp;=(3)(4)/2 = 6\\\\ X&amp;=(3)(3)/2=4.5\\\\ Y&amp;=(4)(4)/2 = 8\\\\ Z&amp;=(5)(5)/2 = 12.5 \\end{align*}\" width=\"163\" height=\"93\" />\n</p><p>Plugging into the answer choices, the only that works is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/0/180806f6ce17fdeccadc834b733c357c2606d408.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}\\ X+Y=Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"136\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Looking at the diagram, we notice that three right isosceles triangles on one right triangle reminds us of the Pythagorean theorem, since each right isosceles triangle is actually half of a square. Each square's area represents a side length squared, so the squares on the legs of the right triangle adds to the square on the hypotenuse. This gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0dd39b6084fca8cebe18092026a45c677c7884a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2X+2Y=2Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"118\" height=\"14\" />. Then, dividing by 2 we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/a/16a74d13cc085d4dd49adcc7b87c7decf5aa97f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X+Y=Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"93\" height=\"16\" />, which is one of the answer choices. Since there can only be one correct answer, and there is already one, we see that the answer must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/0/180806f6ce17fdeccadc834b733c357c2606d408.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}\\ X+Y=Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"136\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/C505IVpZEMc\">https://youtu.be/C505IVpZEMc</a> Soo, DRMS, NM\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ClBrpurT0NQ\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ClBrpurT0NQ</a>  ~David\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_WhyMath\">Video Solution by WhyMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by WhyMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/eJS_P89yKvU\">https://youtu.be/eJS_P89yKvU</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_8\" title=\"2002 AMC 8\">2002 AMC 8</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_AMC_8_Problems\" title=\"2002 AMC 8 Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/i